WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.920 --> 00:00:02.360
Let’s hear it for ears!

00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:04.920
As unique to us
as our fingerprints are,

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:08.200
these curly, bendy bits
of cartilage - called pinna -

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:10.960
have grown out the side of our heads
for good reason.

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:14.160
Back in the day, they helped us hunt
by pinpointing which direction

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:16.240
that rustling in the bushes
was coming from.

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:18.040
But perhaps most importantly,

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:21.000
ears help us communicate
with other human beings. 

00:00:21.640 --> 00:00:23.200
Even before we emerge from the womb,

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:25.360
we’ve decided whose voice
we like most -

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:27.360
it’s our mammy’s!

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:29.880
And we’ve probably had a wee
jive to her favourite songs,

00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:32.160
because our lugs
are already listening

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:33.840
when we’re minus 20 weeks old.

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:38.240
After we’re born, our sticky-out ears
become even more useful.

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:40.480
They deflect sound
towards your eardrum,

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:42.880
keep your glasses from falling
straight off your head,

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:44.720
they help us to socialise, too.

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:48.080
The inner workings of the ear heighten
the frequencies of the human voice -

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:51.600
so much so, that some scientists claim
that listening to someone

00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:55.760
is a more effective way to recognise
and decode complex human emotions

00:00:55.840 --> 00:00:57.600
than looking at their
facial expressions.

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:02.400
But if all the complicated goings-on
seem to happen inside the ear,

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:04.440
why do they stick out
the sides of our heads

00:01:04.520 --> 00:01:06.280
instead of sitting on top
like a bunny,

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:07.800
or hanging low like an elephant? 

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:12.360
It often comes down to whether
an animal is predator or prey.

00:01:12.920 --> 00:01:15.560
Predators, like foxes,
have ears on the top of their heads

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:18.480
that they can point down
at the ground when hunting.

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:22.560
This allows them to hear what poor wee
animals are burrowing underground,

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:25.240
dig down to get them, snack away.

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:27.240
For animals that don’t need to hunt,

00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:30.400
ears on the tops of their heads
allow for better movement.

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:34.360
A rabbit’s ears can swivel
270 degrees

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:36.680
and allow them to hear
for up to 3km -

00:01:36.760 --> 00:01:39.760
meaning that they can be alert for
predators while looking at dinner. 

00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:42.040
Our old pal the African elephant

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:44.440
has the biggest ears
of any creature on Earth,

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:47.240
making up about a fifth
of their surface area.

00:01:47.320 --> 00:01:49.520
They use their giant lugs
to fan themselves,

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:53.400
while the thin skin does
a great job of dissipating heat.

00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:56.680
Our external ears may not serve
us so well on a hot day,

00:01:56.760 --> 00:01:59.320
but they can be an indicator
of our health,

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:01.240
and in the most peculiar way.

00:02:01.720 --> 00:02:04.440
It comes in the form of a diagonal
crease on your earlobe

00:02:04.520 --> 00:02:06.560
called Frank's Sign.

00:02:06.760 --> 00:02:09.560
Benign as it may look,
the appearance of Frank’s sign

00:02:09.640 --> 00:02:13.560
has been linked to cardiovascular
disease and diabetes.

00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:15.960
But scientists are stumped
over why the crease appears

00:02:16.040 --> 00:02:17.920
and how it correlates
to these diseases,

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:20.480
just that it kind of... does.

00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:23.160
Now, while we’re on the subject
of earlobes,

00:02:23.240 --> 00:02:26.520
decorating them is nothing new,
for humans at least.

00:02:26.600 --> 00:02:32.320
The oldest mummy ever found in Europe
- Otzi the Iceman - had pierced ears.

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:35.920
In modern times,
because we’re so damn edgy,

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:38.880
we’ve found about 14 different
ways to pierce our ears,

00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:40.280
each with their own name.

00:02:40.360 --> 00:02:42.760
The most fun of the bunch
might be tragus.

00:02:43.320 --> 00:02:47.280
The word comes from the Ancient Greek
tragos, meaning goat,

00:02:47.360 --> 00:02:49.720
apparently because the tuft of hair
some people have coming

00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:52.400
from their ears looks
like a goat’s beard.

00:02:52.720 --> 00:02:55.280
If the idea of piercings
makes you wince,

00:02:55.520 --> 00:02:57.600
cover your ears now and
be glad you still have them,

00:02:57.680 --> 00:03:01.640
because if you were a criminal
in 16th Century England,

00:03:01.720 --> 00:03:04.800
there was a chance your
punishment would be cropping.

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:08.080
That’s where your ears were cut off
to show the rest of the town

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:09.800
who the delinquent was.

00:03:09.880 --> 00:03:12.720
You were even prescribed a haircut,
so you couldn't hide your shame.

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:16.600
For the uncropped,
our ears never stop growing.

00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:19.040
Maybe good news if you’ve got
eensy-weensy ears,

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:22.120
but for the already large-lugged,
they ain’t going away

00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:25.760
and gravity is only going to
make them get floppier as you age.

00:03:26.440 --> 00:03:29.480
Big or small, it’s the shape
and folds of your ear,

00:03:29.560 --> 00:03:31.680
and how the brain processes
sound waves,

00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:33.840
that help us determine
where a sound is coming from.

00:03:34.120 --> 00:03:36.480
So next time you lose your phone
under the sofa,

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:39.440
it's your twisty ear folds
that are helping you to locate

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:41.120
that Coldplay ringtone.

00:03:41.320 --> 00:03:43.240
Such sounds might be painful to hear -

00:03:43.320 --> 00:03:46.320
physically even,
if they’re over 130 decibels -

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:50.080
but our strange wee ears
give us lots to be thankful for.

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:52.760
They allow us to hear our loved ones,
and best of all,

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:56.360
pretend not to hear them when you
said you’d hang the washing out.

